(7230685, 1117079, 121362, '<td valign="top">Thanks for votes and comments. This night I got the biggest aurora show of the whole winter. Something to remember.</td>', 'Thanks for votes and comments. This night I got the biggest aurora show of the whole winter. Something to remember.', '2014-06-18 18:51:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(7230700, 1116848, 17203, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Nobody:</b></div><hr/><i>''''[You may:] add a border to the outside edge of your entry. Your border must be distinct and clearly recognizable as a border."</i></td></tr></table> <br/>This part is relevant to the border. The frame goes around the outside edge, it''s obviously a border, no image area was added (the frame doesn''t match the background and was within the captured area), and no text, clip art or parts of other photographs were added. That concludes the rules on borders, even in Basic, and we''ve validated multiple breakout borders. I used one all the way back in 2004 and no one even questioned it.<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Nobody:</b></div><hr/><i>"YOU MAY NOT use ANY editing technique to create new image area, objects or features (such as lens flare or motion) that didn''t already exist in your original capture(s)."</i></td></tr></table> <br/>This part is relevant to the CAPTURE, not the border.</td>', 'Originally posted by Nobody:''''[You may:] add a border to the outside edge of your entry. Your border must be distinct and clearly recognizable as a border." This part is relevant to the border. The frame goes around the outside edge, it''s obviously a border, no image area was added (the frame doesn''t match the background and was within the captured area), and no text, clip art or parts of other photographs were added. That concludes the rules on borders, even in Basic, and we''ve validated multiple breakout borders. I used one all the way back in 2004 and no one even questioned it. Originally posted by Nobody:"YOU MAY NOT use ANY editing technique to create new image area, objects or features (such as lens flare or motion) that didn''t already exist in your original capture(s)." This part is relevant to the CAPTURE, not the border.', '2014-06-18 19:48:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(7230708, 1116922, 100831, '<td valign="top">I see it now.</td>', 'I see it now.', '2014-06-18 20:36:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(7230727, 1117114, 16216, '<td valign="top">I definitely would have given this a Plus 10 ...From the Composition to the lighting to the excellent B&amp;W editing. This is truly a beautiful shot..</td>', 'I definitely would have given this a Plus 10 ...From the Composition to the lighting to the excellent B&W editing. This is truly a beautiful shot..', '2014-06-18 21:11:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(7230742, 1117105, 117534, '<td valign="top">Oh I saw this in hidden gem and meant to comment then, but duhhh.... Again You nailed a great composition by the use of negative space, His feathers are soft and fluffy like every good bird should be! Nicely done</td>', 'Oh I saw this in hidden gem and meant to comment then, but duhhh.... Again You nailed a great composition by the use of negative space, His feathers are soft and fluffy like every good bird should be! Nicely done', '2014-06-18 22:50:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(7230749, 1116010, 121111, '<td valign="top">Thanks for all the congratulations , appreciate you take the time to leave a message !</td>', 'Thanks for all the congratulations , appreciate you take the time to leave a message !', '2014-06-18 23:05:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(7230764, 1117135, 38162, '<td valign="top">composition is spot on - it all works. great job.  the hacks at DPC that show us their outtakes from mega budget shoots have no clue about street genre.  stay with it.</td>', 'composition is spot on - it all works. great job.  the hacks at DPC that show us their outtakes from mega budget shoots have no clue about street genre.  stay with it.', '2014-06-19 00:44:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(7230769, 1116848, 45524, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by scalvert:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Nobody:</b></div><hr/><i>''''[You may:] add a border to the outside edge of your entry. Your border must be distinct and clearly recognizable as a border."</i></td></tr></table> <br/>This part is relevant to the border. The frame goes around the outside edge, it''s obviously a border, no image area was added (the frame doesn''t match the background and was within the captured area), and no text, clip art or parts of other photographs were added. That concludes the rules on borders, even in Basic, and we''ve validated multiple breakout borders. I used one all the way back in 2004 and no one even questioned it.<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Nobody:</b></div><hr/><i>"YOU MAY NOT use ANY editing technique to create new image area, objects or features (such as lens flare or motion) that didn''t already exist in your original capture(s)."</i></td></tr></table> <br/>This part is relevant to the CAPTURE, not the border. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>None of that answers my question regarding the cutout(selection with background removal at the paw) or the drop shadow added below it. None of those are allowed in anything other than expert.</td>', 'Originally posted by scalvert: Originally posted by Nobody:''''[You may:] add a border to the outside edge of your entry. Your border must be distinct and clearly recognizable as a border." This part is relevant to the border. The frame goes around the outside edge, it''s obviously a border, no image area was added (the frame doesn''t match the background and was within the captured area), and no text, clip art or parts of other photographs were added. That concludes the rules on borders, even in Basic, and we''ve validated multiple breakout borders. I used one all the way back in 2004 and no one even questioned it. Originally posted by Nobody:"YOU MAY NOT use ANY editing technique to create new image area, objects or features (such as lens flare or motion) that didn''t already exist in your original capture(s)." This part is relevant to the CAPTURE, not the border.   None of that answers my question regarding the cutout(selection with background removal at the paw) or the drop shadow added below it. None of those are allowed in anything other than expert.', '2014-06-19 01:12:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(7230797, 1116778, 133845, '<td valign="top">Looks like the 127 Hours canyon :) Btw a great movie and wonderful photo! ;)</td>', 'Looks like the 127 Hours canyon :) Btw a great movie and wonderful photo! ;)', '2014-06-19 07:40:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(7230834, 1116848, 17203, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by MattO:</b></div><hr/><i>None of that answers my question regarding the cutout(selection with background removal at the paw) or the drop shadow added below it. None of those are allowed in anything other than expert. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>You''re wrong on both counts. Borders generally fall outside of the normal editing rules (you can''t otherwise make selections in Basic, for example) as long as the frame is distinct and goes around the outside, and no text, clip art or other photographs were added. If "background removal" applied to borders, then EVERY border would be illegal, and breakout borders have been validated multiple times outside of Expert editing, including this one.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2014-06-19 10:41:43.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by MattO:None of that answers my question regarding the cutout(selection with background removal at the paw) or the drop shadow added below it. None of those are allowed in anything other than expert.  You''re wrong on both counts. Borders generally fall outside of the normal editing rules (you can''t otherwise make selections in Basic, for example) as long as the frame is distinct and goes around the outside, and no text, clip art or other photographs were added. If "background removal" applied to borders, then EVERY border would be illegal, and breakout borders have been validated multiple times outside of Expert editing, including this one.', '2014-06-19 10:38:36.000000', '2014-06-19 11:41:43.000000', 0),
(7230844, 1116747, 20799, '<td valign="top">I''m sure I''ve faved this before :-).</td>', 'I''m sure I''ve faved this before :-).', '2014-06-19 11:19:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(7230855, 1116848, 45524, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by scalvert:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by MattO:</b></div><hr/><i>None of that answers my question regarding the cutout(selection with background removal at the paw) or the drop shadow added below it. None of those are allowed in anything other than expert. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>You''re wrong on both counts. Borders generally fall outside of the normal editing rules (you can''t otherwise make selections in Basic, for example) as long as the frame is distinct and goes around the outside, and no text, clip art or other photographs were added. If "background removal" applied to borders, then EVERY border would be illegal, and breakout borders have been validated multiple times outside of Expert editing, including this one. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I could show you many "borders" that were DQed including this one <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=696983" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/888/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_696983.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/888/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_696983.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/888/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_696983.jpg"/></a> to show you that Borders do infact fall inside the normal editing rules. That is a faded border that was De''d and there are many others that have been for "creating things that weren''t in the original capture"</td>', 'Originally posted by scalvert: Originally posted by MattO:None of that answers my question regarding the cutout(selection with background removal at the paw) or the drop shadow added below it. None of those are allowed in anything other than expert.  You''re wrong on both counts. Borders generally fall outside of the normal editing rules (you can''t otherwise make selections in Basic, for example) as long as the frame is distinct and goes around the outside, and no text, clip art or other photographs were added. If "background removal" applied to borders, then EVERY border would be illegal, and breakout borders have been validated multiple times outside of Expert editing, including this one.   I could show you many "borders" that were DQed including this one  to show you that Borders do infact fall inside the normal editing rules. That is a faded border that was De''d and there are many others that have been for "creating things that weren''t in the original capture"', '2014-06-19 12:27:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(7230870, 1116848, 17203, '<td valign="top">That was DQ''d for a vignette in Basic, not a border (it would have been fine in Advanced). "Creating things that weren''t in the original capture" applies to the image area, not the border, since obviously the border itself wasn''t in the original capture. I already posted an example of a validated breakout border that ribboned in Advanced. It''s legal, end of story.</td>', 'That was DQ''d for a vignette in Basic, not a border (it would have been fine in Advanced). "Creating things that weren''t in the original capture" applies to the image area, not the border, since obviously the border itself wasn''t in the original capture. I already posted an example of a validated breakout border that ribboned in Advanced. It''s legal, end of story.', '2014-06-19 12:53:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(7230897, 1116848, 96751, '<td valign="top">What a beauty!</td>', 'What a beauty!', '2014-06-19 13:43:14.000000', NULL, 0),
